The changes look good. Now let me reconsider one thing: the test assertions rely on the exact serialization of the pbxproj file reference: `/* Sentry.xcframework */ = {isa = PBXFileReference;`.

Unity's serialization of a PBXFileReference:
```
<24-hex-guid> /* Sentry.xcframework */ = {isa = PBXFileReference; lastKnownFileType = wrapper.xcframework; name = ...; path = ...; sourceTree = ...; };
```

The match string `/* Sentry.xcframework */ = {isa = PBXFileReference;` — matches exactly the start of the file reference definition. 

One concern: could `AddFile` add the framework to a group as well, creating a second occurrence of `/* Sentry.xcframework */ = {isa = PBXFileReference;`? No — groups use `guid /* Sentry.xcframework */,` without `= {isa =`. So unique. Good.

Now, is `ContainsFileByProjectPath` the right method, and does it exist in Unity 2019.4? Yes, `public bool ContainsFileByProjectPath(string path)` is a well-known public method on PBXProject, present since Unity 2017.

But wait — I should double check the semantics of `ContainsFileByProjectPath`. Unity's implementation:

```csharp
public bool ContainsFileByProjectPath(string path)
{
    path = FixSlashesInPath(path);
    return GetFileGUIDsByProjectPath(...)...
}
```

Actually let me recall the real implementation:
```csharp
public bool ContainsFileByProjectPath(string path)
{
    path = FixSlashesInPath(path);
    foreach (var fileRef in m_Section[PBXFileRef.Name])
    { ... }
}
```

Hmm, I'm not 100% certain of implementation details, but the public contract: "Returns true only if the project contains the given file path." It searches file references by their project path. Since we add with the same projectPath, the check will match on subsequent builds.

Actually, let me double check this from the actual UnityPBXProject source. In Unity's `UnityEditor.iOS.Xcode.PBXProject`:

